You are not required to comply with both. Choose whichever you prefer.

The corresponding SPDX expression, as declared in Cargo.toml and in the
per-file headers, is:

    MIT OR Apache-2.0

Why two licenses
----------------

Apache-2.0 grants everything MIT does and adds an explicit patent grant
(section 3), which protects downstream users from patent litigation by
contributors. MIT is shorter and more permissive about attribution. Offering
both is the Rust ecosystem convention, so comply composes without friction
with the crates around it.

Documentation
-------------

Documentation is licensed separately, under the Creative Commons Attribution
3.0 Unported license or any later version (CC-BY-3.0+). Full text:
CC-BY-3.0.txt (a symlink to LICENSES/CC-BY-3.0.txt).

Creative Commons is the standard for prose rather than code, and a separate
documentation license is required by the OpenSSF CII Best Practices badge.

Contribution
------------

Unless you state otherwise, any contribution you intentionally submit for
inclusion in this work, as defined in the Apache-2.0 license, shall be
dual-licensed as above, with no additional terms or conditions.

Contributions are certified under the Developer Certificate of Origin; see
DCO.txt and CONTRIBUTING.md.

Per-file licensing
------------------

Every file in this repository declares its own licensing, either in an SPDX
header or through an annotation in REUSE.toml, following the REUSE
Specification. Run `comply lint` to verify, or consult REUSE.toml for the
aggregate rules.
